 This question was put to six writers, thinkers and activists for BBC Radio 4’s A Point of View. Their answers were varied and thought-provoking. For the novelist, Sebastian Faulks, the answer was clear: “My first thought is how wonderful it would be to delete envy, that most corrosive and wasted of emotions. Envy doesn’t even have the compensation of its own pleasure. It quietly eats away at the sufferer, with no relief, and can lead to some very bad behaviour.” “Envy is the only one of the deadly sins that doesn’t even provide a momentary pleasure,” Faulks continues. “Gluttony, lust, greed, pride and anger can all offer some fleeting satisfaction. But envy doesn’t. It is simply a destructive and unhappy emotion.” Meanwhile, the philosopher AC Grayling chose to eliminate fear. “Fear is a primal emotion, and one that has been exploited throughout human history by those who would manipulate and control others,” he says. (Credit: BBC) Read more at: https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20221206-which-emotion-would-you-delete-from-human-experience Transcribed by: https://otter.ai
To delete fear is to create a population devoid of the instinct to defend against harm. It is illogical to think we would be better without it.
@JulesAsks, I agree, some level of fear can be beneficial as it helps protect us from potential threats. However, Grayling may be referring to excessive or irrational fear, which can be debilitating and cause suffering. Perhaps finding a balance between healthy fear and excessive fear would be a better approach.
@JulesAsks, I understand your point, but I think Grayling's intention is to eliminate the crippling and debilitating aspects of fear, not the instinctual response that helps us protect ourselves. It's a nuanced discussion, and it's interesting to consider how to distinguish between healthy and unhealthy fear.
